{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 20, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "*highlighted section part 1*  \nRaymond III was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. He was born in 1140 and was the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem. His early life was marked by political instability in the Crusader states. He inherited the county while still a minor and was placed under the guardianship of his mother and later under the regency of the King of Jerusalem. During his minority the County of Tripoli faced threats from Muslim forces and internal disputes among the nobility. Raymond III was eventually declared of age in 1156 and began to rule independently. He quickly proved himself to be a capable diplomat and military leader. His rule was characterized by efforts to strengthen alliances with other Crusader states and to maintain peace with neighboring Muslim powers when possible.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*  \nRaymond III played a central role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He was a close ally and sometimes rival of King Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy. Raymond was named regent of Jerusalem in 1174 after the death of Baldwin III and before Baldwin IV came of age. His regency was marked by attempts to stabilize the kingdom but was also plagued by factionalism among the nobles. He was removed from the regency in 1176 by the Haute Cour due to accusations of incompetence and overcaution. Despite this he remained a powerful noble and continued to influence royal decisions. Raymond was also involved in the succession crisis that followed Baldwin IVs death and supported the claim of Baldwin V the young son of Sibylla.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*  \nRaymond III was present at the pivotal Battle of Hattin in 1187 where the Crusader army was crushed by Saladin. He was among the few nobles who survived the battle and escaped to Tripoli. After the fall of Jerusalem and much of the Holy 